The rows are first created while the star count grows. Then the same rows are printed in reverse order, skipping the middle row so it is not duplicated. This keeps the code close to the way the pattern is drawn on paper.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern9(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        String[] upperRows = new String[n];

        for (int level = 1; level <= n; level++) {
            StringBuilder row = new StringBuilder();

            for (int space = 0; space < n - level; space++) {
                row.append(' ');
            }
            for (int star = 0; star < 2 * level - 1; star++) {
                row.append('*');
            }

            upperRows[level - 1] = row.toString();
            System.out.println(upperRows[level - 1]);
        }

        for (int level = n - 2; level >= 0; level--) {
            System.out.println(upperRows[level]);
        }
    }
}
```

For the edge case `n = 1`, the second loop starts at `-1`, so the single middle row is printed just once. Storing the upper rows makes the mirror step simple, but it uses more memory than printing each row directly.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` including the characters written to the output.
Space complexity: `O(n^2)` because the rows are stored as strings.

A diamond has two parts. The top grows from one star to `2 * n - 1` stars, then the bottom shrinks again. Each row gets spaces before its stars so the shape stays centered.

For `n = 3`, the star counts are `1, 3, 5, 3, 1`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void pattern9(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        for (int row = 1; row <= n; row++) {
            for (int space = 1; space <= n - row; space++) {
                cout << " ";
            }
            for (int star = 1; star <= 2 * row - 1; star++) {
                cout << "*";
            }
            cout << "\n";
        }

        for (int row = n - 1; row >= 1; row--) {
            for (int space = 1; space <= n - row; space++) {
                cout << " ";
            }
            for (int star = 1; star <= 2 * row - 1; star++) {
                cout << "*";
            }
            cout << "\n";
        }
    }
};
```

For `n = 1`, only one star is printed. Values less than or equal to zero produce no output. The method only prints the pattern and does not change any input data.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because all rows and their columns are visited.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the output.

This version uses recursion to print the growing half first and the shrinking half after it. The recursive call changes the star count by one, so the two sides of the diamond are easy to see in the code.

```java
class Solution {
    public void pattern9(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        printRows(n, 1);
    }

    private void printRows(int n, int stars) {
        printRow(n, stars);

        if (stars < n) {
            printRows(n, stars + 1);
            printRow(n, stars);
        }
    }

    private void printRow(int n, int stars) {
        for (int space = 0; space < n - stars; space++) {
            System.out.print(" ");
        }

        for (int count = 0; count < 2 * stars - 1; count++) {
            System.out.print("*");
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}
```

The stopping case is the middle row, where `stars == n`. After that row has been printed, the earlier star counts are printed while the recursion returns. A non-positive `n` is handled before recursion starts.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` due to the total number of printed spaces and stars.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursion stack.

Instead of printing only the useful part of each row, this uses a fixed grid of width `2 * n - 1`. A column is a star when it lies inside the star range for the current row. All other positions are spaces.

This makes the centering rule visible. For `n = 3`, the grid width is 5. The star ranges are columns `3`, then `2..4`, then `1..5`, and then they close again.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void pattern9(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return;
        }

        int width = 2 * n - 1;

        for (int row = 1; row <= width; row++) {
            int level = min(row, width - row + 1);
            int firstStar = n - level + 1;
            int lastStar = n + level - 1;

            for (int column = 1; column <= width; column++) {
                if (column >= firstStar && column <= lastStar) {
                    cout << "*";
                } else {
                    cout << " ";
                }
            }
            cout << "\n";
        }
    }
};
```

The middle column is always the center of the diamond. Rows above and below it use the same calculation, so there is no separate lower-half loop. The input value is only read, not modified.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because the full square grid is checked.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

